  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1020 AM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nIt will be another hot day for the Borderland, but with the upper-\nlevel high drifting slightly to the west, temperatures will be \nslightly cooler than yesterday. Granted, it is still hot enough to \nwarrant a Heat Advisory for the Rio Grande Valley east of El Paso, \nTX until 9 PM MDT tonight. For most of the lowlands, surface winds \nwill prevail from the east-southeast at 5-10 mph. The exception is \nthe mountains (particularly the Sacramento Mountains), where \nisolated (25-35%) thunderstorms, by way of orographic lift, could \nproduce outflow winds up to 50 mph later in the afternoon. The \nlatest mesoscale models show some thunderstorm-generated outflows \nhitting El Paso and western Hudspeth Counties in the overnight \nhours, though they should not produce any meaningful precipitation.\n\nThe most recent GFS and ECMWF models have PWAT values around 1.2\" \nfor Thursday, and the latest mesoscale and NBM runs have spotty \nthunderstorms across the lowlands Thursday afternoon. Friday's PWAT \nvalues are about the same, but the best chance for rain and \nthunderstorms on Friday will be in the mountains. Friday will also \nsee lowland temperatures cool to at or just below the 100-degree \nmark. As the weekend rolls in, the ridge will move to our east, \nsetting up a strong monsoonal pattern. The catch is that most of the \nmoisture on Saturday and Sunday will be limited to the Arizona-New \nMexico border, which means scattered showers and thunderstorms west \nof the Continental Divide. The lowlands will remain warm with \ntemperatures running 4-8 degrees above normal, but some of the Rio \nGrande Valley through southern NM could see some isolated storms.\n\nAs it stands, we are seeing a West Coast trough coming in early next \nweek, which will give western New Mexico a good shot of monsoonal \nrain. But by late next week, the extended models diverge as to how \nmuch moisture the area will see based on the strength of the trough. \nThe GFS is trending wetter for the Borderland late next week, while \nthe ECMWF is trending drier (which is what we currently expect to \noccur). We will continue to monitor which track the models will \neventually agree on.",
